      "id": "thm-category-o-translation-intertwines-residue-induction-and-restriction",
      "concern": "The statement identifies f_i^H with the functors of def-hecke-induction-and-restriction-functors-on-degenerate-cyclotomic-blocks, whose initial definition uses the full cyclotomic tower. Proof 1.2 instead proves induction through B_d and B_(d+1), the tensor image algebras. The statement must explicitly select compatible supported functors or an allowed residue interval; replacing image induction by full induction is false. For λ=(1,1), c=0, take M=Λ²C² in degree 2. Its Schur image is the S_2 sign module. Full induction to C[S_3] has dimension 3, with standard and sign constituents, whereas π_3(M⊗C²) is the dimension-2 standard Specht. The residue −2 part of full induction is the nonzero S_3 sign module, which the small tensor representation kills; the corresponding gl_2 tensor translate is zero. I checked directly that the degree-3 antisymmetrizer vanishes on (C²)^⊗3. Brundan–Kleshchev, https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0605217, printed pp. 32–33, Lemma 5.16 and its complete proof, use induction between H_d(λ,c) image algebras. This is an interface qualification requiring adjudication, especially if the intended source residue range already excludes −2.",
